 \section{Discussion and conclusion}
 \label{sec:discussion}
 
-AERead evaluates economic activities through the value an agent can
-deliver for a principal using the information, resources and authority
-available to its seat. The worked examples connect information
-acquisition, bargaining and commitment to economic outcomes. References
-distinguish value attainable by feasible policies, optimal decisions
-under available information, and full-information bounds. Their units
-and counterpart conditions remain explicit so that each reported
-shortfall has a defined interpretation.
+\subsection{What the cases show}
+\label{sec:discussion-findings}
 
-The cases also show why task construction and quality control belong in
-the evaluation method. Briefs can disclose decisive rules, simple
-policies can exhaust a pack's difficulty, and counterparts can determine
-which opportunities a subject encounters. The checks in Section 6
-changed the interpretation of our own results. Trajectory-based loss
-categories locate observed shortfalls; explaining their causes requires
-controlled follow-up comparisons. Replay establishes consistency with
-recorded play, while construct validity requires evidence about the
-worlds, information and controls.
+AERead evaluates an agent's economic decisions by the value it obtains for
+a principal, given the information, resources and authority of its seat.
+Each score is measured against a reference whose access to information is
+stated, whether a feasible policy, the best policy under the seat's
+information or a full-information bound, and it keeps its own units and
+names the counterparts it was measured against, so that every shortfall
+has a defined interpretation. The worked examples apply this to
+information acquisition, bargaining and commitment in housing, procurement
+and contracting, and a reimplementation of part of TERMS-Bench runs on the
+same runtime.
 
-Separate, versioned reanalyses now regenerate the corrected Housing
-measures and Procurement decision losses from sealed records. The
-accompanying projections support score regeneration; full verification
-against original execution still requires retained logs (Appendix G).
-These post-run checks strengthen the results' provenance. They do not
-retroactively qualify the campaigns or resolve Housing's historical
-seat attribution (Appendix H).
+The cases teach three lessons that bear on any evaluation of this kind.
+The counterpart is part of the measurement: the same two agents differ by
+far more when they negotiate with each other than when each faces a fixed
+counterpart. The brief is part of the case: stating a rule it had left
+implicit moved scores as much as changing the agent did. And the defects
+that matter are often properties of the population of worlds, the brief or
+the analysis rather than of any single world, which is why construction
+and quality control belong in the method; the checks of Section 6 changed
+the interpretation of our own results. Loss categories locate a shortfall
+but do not explain it, and explaining it requires controlled follow-up
+comparisons. Replay establishes consistency with recorded play; construct
+validity requires evidence about the worlds, the information and the
+controls.
 
-Procurement and Data center are grounded in realistic purchasing and
-contracting decisions. The present experiments use controlled scenarios;
-empirical calibration of their parameter distributions and transfer to
-field settings remain to be established. Coverage is limited to two
-transaction classes and a small set of agent configurations, with no
-human policy baseline. Several qualification checks were added after
-the runs, and hosted-model and CLI behavior limits attribution from
-declared settings. These examples do not establish general model
-rankings or deployment readiness.
+These properties matter most where agents are deployed. Organizations that
+pilot agents often stop because the business value is unclear and the
+risks are hard to control \citep{gartner2025agentic}; a value score against
+a stated reference and a typed account of losses address both. Small firms
+face a different problem: short of staff \citep{nfib2026jobs}, they often
+leave decisions like those our cases model to one person, without
+specialized expertise and with no review before a commitment. Delegating
+such a decision to an agent is attractive there, and trusting it requires
+evidence that the agent keeps value against the counterparts it will meet.
 
-Future campaigns should bind actual seat assignments and headline
-scorers into frozen evaluation identities and run the required controls
-before comparisons. Practitioner review, human baselines and externally
